      "text": "We start by analyzing the given expression \\(\\frac{1}{\\cos 80^\\circ} - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sin 80^\\circ}\\).\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the expression using a common denominator:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{\\cos 80^\\circ} - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sin 80^\\circ} = \\frac{\\sin 80^\\circ - \\sqrt{3} \\cos 80^\\circ}{\\cos 80^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to simplify the numerator \\(\\sin 80^\\circ - \\sqrt{3} \\cos 80^\\circ\\). Notice that this expression can be rewritten in the form of a single trigonometric function. We recognize that it matches the sine of a specific angle. Specifically, we use the identity for the sine of a sum:\n\\[\n\\sin(30^\\circ + 50^\\circ) = \\sin 30^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ + \\cos 30^\\circ \\sin 50^\\circ\n\\]\nBut we need to adjust our approach. Instead, we use the angle addition formula in reverse:\n\\[\n\\sin(30^\\circ + 50^\\circ) = \\sin 30^\\circ \\cos 50^\\circ + \\cos 30^\\circ \\sin 50^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} \\cos 50^\\circ + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\sin 50^\\circ\n\\]\nThis does not directly help, so let's try another approach. We use the tangent half-angle substitution. Let \\(\\theta = 50^\\circ\\), then:\n\\[\n\\sin 80^\\circ = \\cos 10^\\circ \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\cos 80^\\circ = \\sin 10^\\circ\n\\]\nThus, the expression becomes:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin 80^\\circ - \\sqrt{3} \\cos 80^\\circ}{\\cos 80^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ} = \\frac{\\sin 80^\\circ - \\sqrt{3} \\cos 80^\\circ}{\\sin 80^\\circ \\cos 80^\\circ}\n\\]\nWe use the identity \\( \\sin 2\\theta = 2 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta \\) and \\(\\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^2 \\theta = 1\\) to simplify further. First, we express \\(\\sin 80^\\circ\\) and \\(\\cos 80^\\circ\\) in terms of \\(\\tan 50^\\circ\\):\n\\[\n\\tan 50^\\circ = \\frac{\\sin 50^\\circ}{\\cos 50^\\circ} = \\sqrt{3}\n\\]\nThus, \\(\\sin 50^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\) and \\(\\cos 50^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2}\\). We use these to find \\(\\sin 80^\\circ\\) and \\(\\cos 80^\\circ\\):\n\\[\n\\sin 80^\\circ = \\cos 10^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{5}+1}{4}, \\quad \\cos 80^\\circ = \\sin 10^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{5}-1}{4}\n\\]\nSubstituting these values back, we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin 80^\\circ - \\sqrt{3} \\cos 80^\\circ}{\\sin 80^\\circ \\cos 80^\\circ} = \\frac{\\frac{\\sqrt{5}+1}{4} - \\sqrt{3} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}-1}{4}}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}+1}{4}\\right) \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}-1}{4}\\right)} = \\frac{\\frac{\\sqrt{5}+1 - \\sqrt{15} + \\sqrt{3}}{4}}{\\frac{5-1}{16}} = \\frac{\\frac{\\sqrt{5}+1 - \\sqrt{15} + \\sqrt{3}}{4}}{\\frac{4}{16}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{5}+1 - \\sqrt{15} + \\sqrt{3}}{1} = \\sqrt{5} + 1 - \\sqrt{15} + \\sqrt{3}\n\\]\nAfter simplification, we find:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]",
