      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the expression \\(\\frac{1}{\\cos 80^\\circ} - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sin 80^\\circ}\\), we will follow these steps:\n\n1. Express the given expression in a common denominator.\n2. Simplify the resulting expression.\n\nThe given expression is:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{\\cos 80^\\circ} - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sin 80^\\circ}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we find a common denominator for the two fractions:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{\\cos 80^\\circ} - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sin 80^\\circ} = \\frac{\\sin 80^\\circ}{\\cos 80^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ} - \\frac{\\sqrt{3} \\cos 80^\\circ}{\\cos 80^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ} = \\frac{\\sin 80^\\circ - \\sqrt{3} \\cos 80^\\circ}{\\cos 80^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ}\n\\]\n\nWe can use the identity \\(\\sin 2A = 2 \\sin A \\cos A\\) to simplify the numerator:\n\\[\n\\sin 80^\\circ - \\sqrt{3} \\cos 80^\\circ = 2 \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 80^\\circ - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cos 80^\\circ \\right) = 2 \\left( \\sin 80^\\circ \\cos 60^\\circ - \\cos 80^\\circ \\sin 60^\\circ \\right) = 2 \\sin (80^\\circ - 60^\\circ) = 2 \\sin 20^\\circ\n\\]\n\nSo the expression becomes:\n\\[\n\\frac{2 \\sin 20^\\circ}{\\cos 80^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ}\n\\]\n\nWe can use the double-angle identity for sine, \\(\\sin 160^\\circ = 2 \\sin 80^\\circ \\cos 80^\\circ\\), and the identity \\(\\sin 2A = 2 \\sin A \\cos A\\) to simplify further:\n\\[\n\\cos 80^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 160^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} \\sin (180^\\circ - 20^\\circ) = \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 20^\\circ\n\\]\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\frac{2 \\sin 20^\\circ}{\\frac{1}{2} \\sin 20^\\circ} = \\frac{2 \\sin 20^\\circ \\cdot 2}{\\sin 20^\\circ} = 4\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of the expression is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{4}\n\\]",
